==================================================================
DAY 12 - PART A: THEORY
Rollbacks and Revision History
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. EVERY ROLLOUT IS A REVISION
------------------------------
Each new pod template = a new ReplicaSet = a new revision number.
The revision is stored on the ReplicaSet as the annotation
  deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: "3"

  k rollout history deploy/web
  k rollout history deploy/web --revision=2    (show that template)


2. CHANGE-CAUSE
---------------
The CHANGE-CAUSE column is empty unless you set the annotation
"kubernetes.io/change-cause" on the Deployment:

  k annotate deploy/web kubernetes.io/change-cause="upgrade to 1.27"

(The old --record flag is deprecated. In GitOps setups the Git commit
history plays this role.)


3. ROLLBACK
-----------
  k rollout undo deploy/web                  previous revision
  k rollout undo deploy/web --to-revision=2  a specific one

Rollback is just another rollout: Kubernetes scales the old
ReplicaSet back up and the current one down, using the same rolling
strategy. The rolled-back revision gets a NEW revision number (the old
number disappears from history).


4. HOW MANY REVISIONS ARE KEPT?
-------------------------------
  spec.revisionHistoryLimit: 10   (default)
Old ReplicaSets beyond this are deleted, and you can't roll back to
them.


5. FAILED ROLLOUTS
------------------
A bad image tag causes new pods to hit ImagePullBackOff. Because of
maxUnavailable, the rollout STOPS and old pods keep serving traffic.
The Deployment does not roll back automatically; it waits.

  spec.progressDeadlineSeconds: 600  (default)
After this, the Deployment condition "Progressing" becomes False with
reason ProgressDeadlineExceeded. CI/CD tools watch this and trigger
the rollback.

"k rollout status" exits with an error in that case: perfect for
scripts and pipelines.


6. A SAFE UPDATE ROUTINE
------------------------
  1. Change the YAML (image tag, never "latest")
  2. k diff -f deploy.yaml
  3. k apply -f deploy.yaml
  4. k rollout status deploy/web --timeout=120s
  5. If it fails: k rollout undo deploy/web, then investigate


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Revisions live on ReplicaSets.
- undo = roll forward to an old template (gets a new number).
- A bad rollout stalls safely; it does not undo itself.
- Use rollout status in scripts; it fails on a stuck rollout.
